Z||000012||Voytek||05-09-2001||10:46 AM||||Flashy<BR>There IS a do-it-yourself kit!  It is sold at Canadian Tire.  The kit is kind of spread out throughout the store, though.  First, you need to go to the isle with Fiberglass.  Pick up a big bucket, just in case.  Then, you need to go to the isle with Hacksaws.  Last, but not least, some kind of glass cutter (probably).  It doesn't come with instructions but I'm sure you'll find a couple of other Fiero people in Calgary who'd love to try that conversion on YOUR car.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000013||Oreif||05-09-2001||11:23 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Voytek:<BR><B>Flashy<BR>There IS a do-it-yourself kit!  It is sold at Canadian Tire.  The kit is kind of spread out throughout the store, though.  First, you need to go to the isle with Fiberglass.  Pick up a big bucket, just in case.  Then, you need to go to the isle with Hacksaws.  Last, but not least, some kind of glass cutter (probably).  It doesn't come with instructions but I'm sure you'll find a couple of other Fiero people in Calgary who'd love to try that conversion on YOUR car.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>If you go to Archie's website, He has directions and explains what needs to be done. Note: the windshield isn't cut. It's laid down 9 degrees. That way if it ever breaks or cracks you can use a stock windshield. The rear and side windows are lexan.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||FLASHY||05-09-2001||02:51 PM||||Ummmm...I think I'll wait Voytek. I can just see the next wind storm turning my "Canadian Tire Choptop" into a "Canadian Tire Convertible". Hey, I guess that's not so bad either...what am I thinking?!<P>No seriously though, I remember reading about the blue choptop a while back where it said there may be a kit in the future that you can buy to do it yourself. That would be cool!||||reg||12
